Lambdas — the syntax.

A lambda has three parts: a parameter list, the arrow, and a body. That's the whole grammar — parameters, arrow, body — but it flexes in a few ways worth knowing, so you can read and write every form fluently.

First, the shapes of the parameter list. With no parameters, you write empty parentheses, arrow, forty-two. With exactly one parameter, you can drop the parentheses — x, arrow, x times two. With several, the parentheses are required — x, y, arrow, x plus y. And you may write explicit types, like int x, int y — but you almost never do, because the compiler infers them. So the rules are: one untyped parameter may drop its parentheses; zero, or two or more, need them; and you can write the types but rarely should. You can also use var for parameters, if you want to attach an annotation.

Next, the two body forms. An expression body has no braces and no return — x, arrow, x times two — and the expression itself is the returned value. That's the common, tidy form. A block body uses braces, and if it returns something, it needs an explicit return — x, arrow, brace, return x times two. You'd use a block when there are multiple statements: print the name, then return its length. Use the expression form whenever the logic fits in a single expression.

Now, the important concept: target typing — where a lambda's type actually comes from. A lambda by itself has no type. It takes its type from context — from the functional interface it's being assigned to, or passed as. So empty-parens, arrow, print "hi", assigned to a runnable, becomes a runnable, matching its run method. The two-argument length comparison, assigned to a comparator of string, matches compare. And empty-parens, arrow, "result", assigned to a callable, matches call. Notice: the identical lambda shape becomes a runnable, or a callable, or anything else with a matching single method, purely depending on where it's used. The compiler checks the lambda's parameters and return against that one abstract method. And this is why a lambda can only appear where a functional-interface type is expected — assign one to a bare var, with no target, and it fails, because there's nothing to infer the type from.

A quick note on the arrow. You met the arrow already, in module one's arrow switch. These are different uses of the same token. In a switch, the arrow separates a label from its result; in a lambda, it separates the parameters from the body. Context tells them apart, so there's no confusion in practice — just don't expect them to be the same feature.

Put it all together, and the everyday forms read at a glance. List dot for-each, item, arrow, print the item — a consumer: one argument, void body. List dot remove-if, s, arrow, s is blank — a predicate: one argument, returning a boolean. List dot sort, a and b, arrow, a compare-to b — a comparator: two arguments, returning an int. And button dot on-click, empty parens, arrow, save — no arguments. Each one is "parameters, arrow, what to do" — the parameters matching the interface's method, the body supplying its logic. And very often, the body is just a call to an existing method — for which there's an even shorter spelling: the method reference, which is next.
